t/project-state.yaml", ".prawduct/runbooks/moving-the-pi-player-to-a-new-revision.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "tv_api_check exits 0 when it declines to select, so a deploy gate run against a set not in art mode passes with the callback check never exercised", "recommendation": "Instance. The new refusal in check_callbacks (tools/tv_api_check.py:341-344) records a 'note', and Report.failed counts only FAIL. So a run with --image against a set showing a programme or in standby, where get_artmode answers rather than raising, exits 0. The module docstring says the exit code 'can gate a deploy', and that gate now passes without callback evidence. The output does give the skip its own line. It does not give it its own exit status. Core learning 'When a check can be quiet for more than one reason, give each quiet state its own output and match the form the tooling parses' applies here. Either return a distinct non-zero exit (e.g. 3, 'not tested: set not in art mode') whenever --image was passed and the selection was declined, or decide that the note is enough and say so in the docstring's exit-code sentence. Not a defect in the safety change itself, which matches SamsungTv.showing_art's rule and is tested on both halves.", "files": ["tools/tv_api_check.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "Recorded suite evidence does not cover 52fc631's new tests (test-status: current, not tree-vouched)", "recommendation": "Instance. prawduct-hook test-status exits 0 but reports that tests/test_tools.py and tools/tv_api_check.py changed after the recorded run (2026-10-27T14:37:45Z, at 7ac249c). So the five new parametrized/positive cases for the art-mode refusal have no recorded pass. By code reading they look correct: the api_check fixture, asyncio_mode=auto, and the patched asyncio.sleep all line up. Rerun `uv run --group raster pytest` before merge so the evidence matches the tree being merged. Under the exit-code rule this is not a WARNING. It is noted because exit 0 here does not mean 'measured at this tree'.", "files": ["tests/test_tools.py", "tools/tv_api_check.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The 'six shared names' list is hand-copied into the citation test and is already incomplete: project-preferences.md exists in both repos, and test_plane_isolation.py cites Arrt's copy bare as 'this repo's'", "recommendation": "Scope: class. CLAUDE.md 'Six names exist in both repositories' and tests/test_artifact_citations.py SHARED_NAMES enumerate the same six by hand. But Arrt also carries project-preferences.md, and an Arrt checkout also has boundary-patterns.md (both are here too). The convention this changeset introduced says 'a bare name means this repo's copy'. tests/test_plane_isolation.py:33-34 breaks it today: 'recorded in `project-preferences.md` as this repo's cautionary example'. That cautionary row is in Arrt's project-preferences.md (row 'The per-wall manifest and its media...', 'This row is this table's cautionary example'). This repo's copy, row 157, has no such text. A maintainer who follows the convention looks here and finds nothing, and the test cannot flag it because the name is not in SHARED_NAMES and the citation has no section. This is the learnings rule 'When a claim already lives in prose, verify it against the code and add a pointer, never a copy': the test copies CLAUDE.md's list, and both copies drifted together. Fix (1): replace the copied list with a derived one. Every name under this repo's .prawduct/artifacts/ is a candidate, since a bare section citation of any local artifact must resolve locally. This also covers the next shared name discovery adds. Fix (2): make CLAUDE.md's count relational (e.g. 'names that exist in both, among them...') or point it at the test. Fix (3): qualify the test_plane_isolation.py citation as Arrt's and correct 'this repo's'. Search: grep the subject files for bare `project-preferences.md` and `boundary-patterns.md`, and check which of the findings artifacts Arrt still carries after the split.", "files": ["tests/test_artifact_citations.py", "CLAUDE.md", "tests/test_plane_isolation.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "tv_api_check hand-copies the daemon's art-mode predicate instead of sharing it", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. check_callbacks writes `mode != \"on\"` and its comment names `SamsungTv.showing_art` as the rule's home. That method's predicate is `mode == \"on\"` plus an unreadable-reply-means-no clause. The tool drives the raw library on purpose, so it cannot call the method. A module-level predicate in arrt_player.tv.samsung (e.g. `is_plainly_art_mode(reply)`), used by both, would keep the tool from drifting if the daemon's reading of the reply ever widens. With one comparison, accepting this as it stands is reasonable.", "files": ["tools/tv_api_check.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "tv_api_check docstring narrates its own history", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. 'Until 2026-10-27 it selected without asking, and this paragraph said it did not change art mode.' is history in a shipped docstring. Per the prose-remedy rule it is a deletion: drop the sentence. The commit message and the observability strategy's closed-items list already carry it. Nothing reads it, and no other edit is needed.", "files": ["tools/tv_api_check.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The 'six shared names' list leaves out project-preferences.md, and a bare citation of Arrt's copy already sits on a line this branch edited", "recommendation": "Why it broke: the list of names both repositories carry was written down twice by hand, in CLAUDE.md ('Six names exist in both repositories') and in tests/test_artifact_citations.py SHARED_NAMES, and nobody checked it against Arrt. The last Arrt checkout on this machine (arrt-displays, 2026-10-09) also has project-preferences.md and boundary-patterns.md, and both are standard files in a Prawduct product, so Arrt almost certainly still has them. That is an inference: Arrt's current tree was not read. One bare citation that means Arrt's copy already exists: .prawduct/artifacts/platform-and-dependency-findings.md line 42, 'Arrt's `operational-spec.md` \u00a7 The Curation Interpreter and `project-preferences.md`, which records the version per plane'. This branch qualified the first name on that line and left the second bare. This repo's project-preferences.md records no per-plane version, so under the new convention a reader opens the wrong file, and the test cannot see it because project-preferences is not in SHARED_NAMES. Following CLAUDE.md, the next agent will cite Arrt's norm index bare. Scope: class. Check Arrt's current .prawduct/artifacts for every name this repo also has, and correct both lists. The fix should be a structure rather than a longer list: have the test require that every file in this repo's .prawduct/artifacts is either in SHARED_NAMES or in an explicit list of files only this repo has. That forces a decision for each new artifact. CLAUDE.md should then point at SHARED_NAMES rather than copy it. Rules: 'When you write a negative claim, run the search that would have falsified it' and 'When a claim already lives in prose, verify it against the code and add a pointer, never a copy'.", "files": ["CLAUDE.md", "tests/test_artifact_citations.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "tv.close_failed now logs at WARNING once per close, outside the episode reporting every other persistent TV trouble uses", "recommendation": "Raising the line from DEBUG to WARNING is right: before, it was never emitted. But _quietly_close runs on every failed connect (two sites in SamsungTv.connect), on every failed _call and on every read_identity. So a set that keeps refusing the channel, which is when a close is most likely to fail, logs one WARNING per retry. The retries are bounded only by the connection backoff, 5 to 300 s. Meanwhile tv.unavailable for the same episode goes through ReportOnce. observability-strategy.md \u00a7 Episodes says a persistent condition is logged when it starts and when it ends, and does not list tv.close_failed among the events logged every time. The volume is bounded and the line is useful, so this is low weight. Scope: instance. Either run it through ReportOnce or state that it is logged every time.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/tv/samsung.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "tv_api_check's module docstring narrates its own history", "recommendation": "'Until 2026-10-27 it selected without asking, and this paragraph said it did not change art mode.' is history inside a shipped docstring. The next round will find it stale, and git log already has it. Scope: instance. Delete the sentence; the present-tense claim before it stands on its own.", "files": ["tools/tv_api_check.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog reconciliation: no open item resolved; open items in areas this diff touched", "recommendation": "Scope: none. 13 open items, from the cache for brookstalley/arrt-player, confirmed 2026-10-27T14:59:53Z. `affecting` for the changed TV and tool files returns 0, and no item reads as resolved by this diff. C-B3: the diff touches areas that have open items nobody updated. tv: #2 (pairing prompt over a programme) and #3 (token file world-readable); src/arrt_player/tv/samsung.py changed. docs: #14 (stale plane wording); suites.yml changed 'display leg' to 'suite job', and the 'display plane cannot start' stderr in __main__.py:389 that #14 names is still there. #10-#13 match the open gaps in observability-strategy.md \u00a7 Known gaps, which is consistent.", "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings cross-check: rules read, and which fired", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read: core.md, plus display.md and tooling.md (from learnings-files --for-diff). Two findings above rest on rules. 'When you write a negative claim, run the search\u2026' and 'When a claim already lives in prose\u2026' back the shared-names finding. No rule in .claude/rules/learnings changed this cycle, and there is no implicit supersession to report. Change-level checks: the new citation test guards itself (test_the_scan_finds_bare_citations), which agrees with 'When a test, lint or check is new or rewritten, watch it fail once'. The tv_api_check tests include the case where the tool does select, which agrees with 'When a clause or notice is conditional\u2026'.", "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Records pass over files_oracle: no record clears a bar on its own", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Covered all 14 oracle files: architecture.md, data-model.md, hdmi-output-findings.md, nonfunctional-requirements.md, observability-strategy.md, operational-spec.md, platform-and-dependency-findings.md, project-preferences.md, samsung-tv-state-findings.md, security-model.md, backlog.md (deleted; the backend is GitHub Issues), changes/stand-alone.md, project-state.yaml and the Pi move runbook. Checked against the tree: operational-spec's unit guards, TimeoutStopSec 90, the CI jobs and commands, the stderr text, runbook steps 12-17 and F7/F9, and the test files it names. Each matches. The one record defect that matters, the bare project-preferences citation in platform-and-dependency-findings.md:42, is folded into the shared-names finding. These clear no bar and need no edit: operational-spec \u00a7 The hand-run tools says the tool docstrings show 'cd arrt-player &&', which tv_api_check.py no longer does (label_preview.py and power_probe.py still do); and observability-strategy's episode exceptions list omits tv.close_failed.", "fid": "R-11"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 2, "note": 9}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 600, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `feature/discovery`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunk": null, "base_reviewed": "c41a91919f0a110a3e5339ab8c330076b2fb2f1f", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/hdmi-output-findings.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/nonfunctional-requirements.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/observability-strategy.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/operational-spec.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/platform-and-dependency-findings.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/project-preferences.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/samsung-tv-state-findings.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/security-model.md", ".prawduct/backlog.md", ".prawduct/changes/stand-alone.md", ".prawduct/project-state.yaml", ".prawduct/runbooks/moving-the-pi-player-to-a-new-revision.md", "CLAUDE.md", "deploy/README.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0}}, "dispatched_at": "2026-10-27T15:01:27Z"}}

